contentText:
Ian &#8220;Molly&#8221; Meldrum will be honored with a state funeral, as tributes pour in from across the music world for the Australian broadcaster and record producer, who died Monday (Nov. 23) in Melbourne at 83.

Kylie Minogue , who appeared on Meldrum&#8217;s Countdown as a teenager at the start of her career, remembered him as one of her earliest inspirations.

&#8220;He championed so many Aussie artists and welcomed international artists to Australia in his own wonderful and inimitable way,&#8221; she wrote. &#8220;I&#8217;m so grateful for his unwavering love and support. Vale Molly. I&#8217;m going to turn the music up in your honour.&#8221;

Jimmy Barnes offered the most complicated tribute, recalling that Meldrum was initially an adversary — &#8220;running the best music TV show in the country but insisting that we had to mime.&#8221; The relationship changed. &#8220;Molly exposed us to a huge music audience we could never have reached, and we were always thankful to him for that,&#8221; Barnes wrote on Instagram.

Tina Arena , a longtime friend, posted a gallery of images. &#8220;I&#8217;m deeply saddened by the passing of Molly Meldrum, a man who made an incredible contribution to the music industry in Australia and abroad,&#8221; she wrote. &#8220;My deepest condolences go out to his family and friends.&#8221;

Iva Davies of Icehouse wrote on X that Meldrum &#8220;was such a bright, larger-than-life figure in our Australian music scene for so many years with positive influence on so many careers via his work on Countdown , as a journalist, producer, advocate and friend.&#8221; He added: &#8220;Like the rest of the music community that owed so much to his support, the band and I are so thankful that Molly was a part of our lives and music.&#8221;

Darren Hayes of Savage Garden wrote that he doesn&#8217;t &#8220;remember a day in my life without Molly Meldrum being a part of it.&#8221;

Jason Donovan called him &#8220;a truly unique character, a great Australian and one of this country&#8217;s greatest pioneers and ambassadors for music and entertainment.&#8221; He added: &#8220;He was loved, recognised and respected around the world for the extraordinary contribution he made to Australian music and the artists he championed. There will never be another quite like him.&#8221;

Michael Bublé called Meldrum &#8220;one of a kind.&#8221; Marcia Hines wrote: &#8220;Can&#8217;t believe that he has gone. I thank him for everything that he did for us all in the Australian music industry, and worldwide. Rest in peace, my friend.&#8221; Leo Sayer bid &#8220;a fond farewell to the wonderful, fun, crazy, knowledgeable, influential and irrepressible soul who will live on in all our memories as long as Rock &#8216;n Roll will.&#8221;

Skyhooks, whom Meldrum helped turn into household names, wrote: &#8220;So sad to get the call this morning that Ian (Molly) Meldrum had passed away. Skyhooks-Molly-Countdown [were] the perfect fit. His love of Aussie music will be his legacy.&#8221; Human Nature remembered him as &#8220;such a huge presence in the Australian music industry&#8221; and &#8220;a great friend and supporter of so many up-and-coming bands and artists — ourselves included.&#8221;

Kate Ceberano was among the first to post. &#8220;They don&#8217;t make em like that anymore,&#8221; she wrote. &#8220;My heart is aching today at the loss.&#8221;

Richard Wilkins, the veteran Australian entertainment reporter who spent decades competing with Meldrum for the same interviews, called him &#8220;completely irreplaceable&#8221; on Nova 96.9 Sydney&#8217;s Ricki-Lee & Tim on Monday morning. &#8220;The word legend and the word icon are thrown around fairly loosely, but he was both of those things,&#8221; Wilkins said. &#8220;He could pick a hit, and he could make a hit.&#8221;

Wilkins recalled record labels sending the pair around the world with a rep &#8220;to make sure Molly didn&#8217;t lose another passport&#8221; — Meldrum&#8217;s tally eventually reached 13. He also shared a story from one of those trips, when Meldrum, handed a bio for a new artist at a record company office in New York, asked aloud, &#8220;Who the f*** is Madonna ?&#8221; — only for the singer herself to reply, &#8220;And who the f*** are you?&#8221;

The Australian Recording Industry Association said Meldrum had spent six decades convincing a country raised on British and American records that its own artists could stand alongside the greats. &#8220;All of us at ARIA are heartbroken,&#8221; chief executive Annabelle Herd said. &#8220;It is impossible to imagine what Australian music would look like without Molly&#8217;s long and storied career. He shaped the music taste of a generation of Australians and gave so much of himself for the good of Australian artists, for which our whole industry is eternally grateful.&#8221;

Herd noted Meldrum had used his 2014 ARIA Hall of Fame induction speech to urge viewers to get behind Australian acts. &#8220;It is up to all of us to keep fighting the good fight in his honour,&#8221; she said.

Meldrum presented Countdown on the ABC from 1974 to 1987, giving ABBA , Madonna and Blondie their first Australian hits. He was inducted into the ARIA Hall of Fame alongside the show in 2014, the first inductees who were not recording artists.
